entry.summary:
Database management is a core skill for any developer, and two of the most powerful tools in SQL are cursors and triggers. While they serve different purposes, both allow for fine-grained control and automation within your database. In this post, we'll walk through a practical example of each using Oracle Live SQL. Part 1: Cursors – Processing a Result Set Row by Row The Task: We want to create a cursor that finds and displays the names of all employees whose salary is greater than $50,000. Step 1: Create the Employee Table SQL CREATE TABLE Employee (
INSERT INTO Employee VALUES (1, 'Ravi', 65000.00); COMMIT;
Step 2: Use a PL/SQL Block to Process the Cursor SQL SET SERVEROUTPUT ON; DECLARE CURSOR c_employee IS SELECT EmployeeName, Salary FROM Employee WHERE Salary > 50000;
BEGIN LOOP FETCH c_employee INTO v_employee_name, v_salary;
EXIT WHEN c_employee%NOTFOUND;
DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Employee: ' || v_employee_name || ' | Salary: ' || v_salary); END LOOP;
CLOSE c_employee;
END;
Part 2: Triggers – Automating Database Events The Task: We want to create an AFTER INSERT trigger on our Students table. Whenever a new student is added, a log entry should be automatically inserted into a separate Student_Audit table. Step 1: Create the Students and Student_Audit Tables SQL -- Create the Students table
Step 2: Create the AFTER INSERT Trigger SQL CREATE OR REPLACE TRIGGER trg_Student_Audit SQL -- Insert the new student records
Finally, we'll query the Student_Audit table to confirm that all four INSERT actions were logged. SQL SELECT * FROM Student_Audit;
Conclusion As you can see, cursors and triggers are incredibly useful for handling specific data-processing tasks and automating operations. They give you the power to go beyond standard SQL statements and build a more robust and responsive database system.
